.TH SRV 3
.SH NAME
srv \- server registry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #s /srv
.nf

.BI /srv/ service1
.BI /srv/ service2
\&...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I srv
device serves a one-level directory of names, each a service something
posted while the machine ran.
Anything that can name
.B /srv
can mount what is posted there.
See
.IR bind (1)
and
.IR bind (2).
.PP
Posting is two steps, the way Plan 9's is.
Creating
.BI /srv/ name
reserves the name and makes a
.IR pending
entry: nothing serves it yet, and a mount of it fails with
.BR ENXIO .
Writing a decimal file descriptor number to the open fid completes it: the
kernel takes the channel behind that descriptor in the calling process's
own table, and from then on the name reaches whatever that descriptor was
open on.
A write of a descriptor to an entry already completed fails with
.BR EPERM ,
because a posted service is not a thing to swap out from under whoever
already mounted its name.
.PP
Removing
.BI /srv/ name
takes the name away, pending or completed, and does not stop the service:
a mount made before the removal keeps working, because it holds the
channel directly rather than the name.
What ends is the ability to make a new mount by that name.
.PP
Reading
.BI /srv/ name
answers one line: the
.B #
name of the device serving it, and whether it has worker threads of its
own or answers directly on the caller's, or the single word
.B pending
for an entry nothing has completed yet.
.SH EXAMPLES
Posting one end of a pipe as a named service:
.IP
.EX
fd = create("/srv/namedpipe", OWRITE, 0666)
fprint(fd, "%d", p[0])
close(fd)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/srv
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR bind (1),
.IR bind (2),
.IR pipe (2),
.IR open (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Creating a name already posted, pending or completed, fails with
.BR EEXIST .
Mounting a name whose entry is still pending fails with
.BR ENXIO .
Writing a descriptor to an already-completed entry fails with
.BR EPERM .
Writing something other than a decimal number fails with
.BR EINVAL .
.SH BUGS
Unlike Plan 9's
.BR /srv ,
reading an entry does not hand back the channel itself: there is no
descriptor to pass through a read, so a read reports the service's
identity in text instead.
